  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 124 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nThe potential for some more active weather arrives Saturday as a \ntrough digs southward across the High Plains and into the central \nUS. With models becoming more consistent, confidence in this system \nand precipitation development continues to increase. At this time, \nmuch of the forecast area should expect to see at least some \nprecipitation, although, amounts are uncertain. The latest \nprobabilities show the potential of receiving at least 0.10 of QPF \nto be near 90 percent for much of western and north central \nNebraska. When looking at the potential of receiving 0.5 of \nsnowfall, there is a bullseye centered over Sheridan and Cherry \ncounty of at least 80 percent. Therefore, current thinking remains \non track that temperatures should be warm enough to support all rain \nfor most of the area. Areas across the Panhandle and Pine Ridge \ncould see some rain/snow mix by the afternoon, changing over to all \nsnow in the evening. As mentioned previously, the changeover of rain \nto snow will depend highly on the speed of the colder air behind the \ntrough versus how fast the precipitation exits the area. If that \ncolder air can push in quicker, snow could be possible further to \nthe east across the Sandhills and into north central Nebraska. \nRegardless, confidence is growing that the Pine ridge will see at \nleast a slushy light accumulation of snow by Sunday morning. There \nis lower confidence in exactly how much accumulations will fall. A \ndry and quiet forecast will return on Sunday lasting through the \nfirst part of next week as upper level ridging builds back across \nthe western US and surface high pressure returns over the Great \nPlains. \n\nTemperatures will return to near normals in the mid 40s to low 50s \nfor the weekend as cool air advection begins to push southward into \nNebraska over the weekend. Temperatures will even dip into the low \nto mid 40s on Sunday as the colder air behind the departing trough \nfilters across the region. Highs return to the mid 40s to low 50s \nfor the beginning of next week as ridging returns. Overnight lows \nwill generally remain above normals through Tuesday night. Expect \nlows in the mid 20s to low 30s which is nearly 5 to 10 degrees above \nnormals (upper teens to low 20s).",
